does  pretty  much  what  it says.  It examines installed package
records in deletes the package contents, and finally removes  the
package  records.   If  a  package is required by other installed
packages, will list those dependent packages and refuse to delete
the package (unless the option is given).  If a package has  been
marked  as  a  package, it will not be able to be deleted (unless
more than one occurrence of the option is given).  If a  filename
is  given  instead  of  a  package name, the package of which the
given file belongs to can be deleted if the flag is  given.   The
filename  needs  to  be  absolute, see the output produced by the
